    "body": "This change might not be web-compatible. I am currently investigating.\r\n\r\nIt turns out that `new WebSocket(url, undefined, {})` is a pattern used in some web applications. I don't yet know how common it is. The popular [ws](https://github.com/websockets/ws) Node.js WebSocket library takes a third-argument with an options bag. I have seen code like\r\n```js\r\nnew WebSocket(url, undefined, isNodeJS() ? { headers } : {});\r\n```\r\nin the wild.\r\n\r\nBefore this change, the above constructor call behaves like `new WebSocket(url, \"undefined\")`. If the server response headers include `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol: undefined` it will work.\r\n\r\nAfter this change, the above constructor call behaves like `new WebSocket(url, {})`, which is functionally equivalent to `new WebSocket(url)`. If the server response headers include `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol: undefined` it will fail the handshake due to the subprotocol mismatch.\r\n\r\nWithout actually trying it, I don't know whether servers will stop returning `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol: undefined` if clients stop requesting it, or continue to send it and fail.",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
